   "id": "2fa75b95",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Preprocessing and Feature Engineering"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4c14a601",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Data cleaning"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "659a497c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "Data cleaning is a key step in preprocessing, aiming to reduce noise and improve consistency in the dataset. This is particularly important in text classification tasks, where user-generated text often contains irrelevant patterns, inconsistencies, and errors that may negatively affect model performance. Therefore, several cleaning steps were applied to ensure that the data is suitable for subsequent analysis and machine learning models. The following cleaning steps were applied to improve data quality: \n",
    "    - Removal of reviews containing unusually long tokens. A heuristic threshold of 20 characters was used to identify unusually long tokens, as words exceeding this length are unlikely to represent typical natural language.\n",
    "    - Removal of reviews with repeated consecutive words (three or more repetitions). \n",
    "    - Basic text normalization (lowercasing, removal of punctuation and numbers) \n",
    "    - Removal of empty reviews after cleaning\n",
    "\n",
    "The cleaning steps were applied in a structured sequence to ensure correct data processing. Text normalization was performed before removing empty reviews, as some observations only become empty after cleaning (e.g., after removing punctuation and numerical characters). This ensures that all non-informative observations are properly identified and removed. The following code implements the data cleaning steps described above. \n",
    "\n",
    "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/data-analysis/what-is-data-cleaning/ Source to what data cleaning is. \n",
    "\n",
    "We chose not to remove duplicated reviews because duplicates can still carry meaningful information about the distribution of the target variable (review scores). Removing them could unintentionally reduce important signals and affect class balance.\n",
    "\n",
    "We also chose not to remove very short reviews because even short texts can contain strong and useful sentiment (e.g., “Great!”, “Terrible service”). Eliminating them could lead to loss of valuable training data and reduce the model’s ability to generalize across different types of input lengths.\n"
   ]
  },
